        {
          "block_label": "header",
          "block_content": "Chapter 1 Matrices and Gaussian Elimination ",
          "block_bbox": [
            1218,
            236,
            2245,
            290
          ],
          "block_id": 1,
          "block_order": null
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(b) Construct a matrix that has column $1+2(column3)=0$ . Check that A is singular (fewer than 3 pivots) and explain why that must be the case.",
          "block_bbox": [
            598,
            397,
            2995,
            565
          ],
          "block_id": 2,
          "block_order": 1
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": " 1.27 True or false, with reason if true and counterexample if false:",
          "block_bbox": [
            432,
            630,
            2376,
            697
          ],
          "block_id": 3,
          "block_order": 2
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(1) If $L_{1}U_{1}=L_{2}U_{2}$ (upper triangular $U^{\\mathrm{{s}}}$ with nonzero diagonal, lower triangular $L^{\\prime}s$ with unit diagonal), then $L_{1}=L_{2}$ and $U_{1}=U_{2}$ .The $L U$ factorization is unique.\n",
          "block_bbox": [
            597,
            768,
            2996,
            1024
          ],
          "block_id": 4,
          "block_order": 3
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(2)$\\operatorname{I f}A^{2}+A=I\\operatorname{t h e n}A^{-1}=A+I.$ ",
          "block_bbox": [
            600,
            1063,
            1632,
            1138
          ],
          "block_id": 5,
          "block_order": 4
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(3) If all diagonal entries of A are zero, then A is singular.",
          "block_bbox": [
            599,
            1185,
            2278,
            1258
          ],
          "block_id": 6,
          "block_order": 5
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "1.28 By experiment or the Gauss-Jordan method compute ",
          "block_bbox": [
            432,
            1325,
            2135,
            1395
          ],
          "block_id": 7,
          "block_order": 6
        },
        {
          "block_label": "formula",
          "block_content": "\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ m&0&1\\end{bmatrix}^n,\\qquad\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ m&0&1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1},\\qquad\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ 0&m&1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}.",
          "block_bbox": [
            1029,
            1450,
            2578,
            1780
          ],
          "block_id": 8,
          "block_order": 7
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "1.29  Write down the 2 by 2 matrices that ",
          "block_bbox": [
            434,
            1851,
            1652,
            1916
          ],
          "block_id": 9,
          "block_order": 8
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(a) reverse the direction of every vector.",
          "block_bbox": [
            604,
            1991,
            1777,
            2056
          ],
          "block_id": 10,
          "block_order": 9
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(b) project every vector onto the x2 axis.",
          "block_bbox": [
            601,
            2108,
            1785,
            2175
          ],
          "block_id": 11,
          "block_order": 10
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(c) turn every vector counterclockwise through 90°.",
          "block_bbox": [
            603,
            2223,
            2105,
            2291
          ],
          "block_id": 12,
          "block_order": 11
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "(d) reflect every vector through the $45^{\\circ}$ line $x_{1}=x_{2}$ ",
          "block_bbox": [
            601,
            2340,
            2107,
            2411
          ],
          "block_id": 13,
          "block_order": 12
        }
      ],
      "markdown": "(b) Construct a matrix that has column $1+2(column3)=0$ . Check that A is singular (fewer than 3 pivots) and explain why that must be the case.\n\n### 1.27 True or false, with reason if true and counterexample if false:\n\n(1) If $L_{1}U_{1}=L_{2}U_{2}$ (upper triangular $U^{\\mathrm{{s}}}$ with nonzero diagonal, lower triangular $L^{\\prime}s$ with unit diagonal), then $L_{1}=L_{2}$ and $U_{1}=U_{2}$ .The $L U$ factorization is unique.\n\n\n\n(2)$\\operatorname{I f}A^{2}+A=I\\operatorname{t h e n}A^{-1}=A+I.$ \n\n(3) If all diagonal entries of A are zero, then A is singular.\n\n### 1.28 By experiment or the Gauss-Jordan method compute \n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ m&0&1\\end{bmatrix}^n,\\qquad\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ m&0&1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1},\\qquad\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\ \\ell&1&0\\\\ 0&m&1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}.$$\n\n### 1.29 Write down the 2 by 2 matrices that \n\n(a) reverse the direction of every vector.\n\n(b) project every vector onto the x2 axis.\n\n(c) turn every vector counterclockwise through 90°.\n\n(d) reflect every vector through the $45^{\\circ}$ line $x_{1}=x_{2}$ ",
